1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,816
The in-app browser gives a clean session.

2
00:00:01,816 --> 00:00:07,000
This episode is for the opposite case — using a Chrome session you're already signed into, via the browser extension.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:14,777
Open chrome colon slash slash extensions, turn on Developer mode in the top right, click Load unpacked, and select the extension folder from this repo.

4
00:00:14,777 --> 00:00:19,000
It appears in your toolbar, and the agent can now drive that actual Chrome window.

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:28,000
When to reach for this instead of the in-app browser: a login already established elsewhere — your email, a dashboard you're signed into — versus a clean, disposable session where you'd rather the agent start from nothing.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,000
Established login, use the extension; clean slate, use the in-app browser.

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:40,409
This is also where tab tools live — they only exist here, not in the in-app browser: open_new_tab, list_tabs, change_tab, and close_tab.

8
00:00:40,409 --> 00:00:42,000
Real Chrome, real tabs.

9
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:52,000
And unlike the in-app browser, this one really can take screenshots — take_screenshot and read_screenshot both work here, since it's a genuine Chrome window the extension can capture.

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,542
The gotcha, and it matters: a signed-in session is delegated access.

11
00:00:54,542 --> 00:01:00,187
If you're signed into your bank, your email, your work systems in that browser, the model can act on any of them — not just the page you pointed it at.

12
00:01:00,187 --> 00:01:04,000
Treat the decision to hand it your session with the same care as handing someone your unlocked laptop.

13
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:10,000
From driving a browser to generating with one — next is images, audio, video, and 3D.

